[%emt 0:08:12] [%think 48200] [%out 48595] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0]
[%cachewrite 2718] Red's advisor on e1 can capture a pawn next move, on either
d2 or f2. No single move protects both pawns, so I push the one that ends up
safer. **Why d2→d1:** - On d1 the pawn covers d0 and e1, so the advisor can no
longer reach it. - If the advisor takes f2, e1 is left empty. My horse on e2
then covers d0 and f0, and the pawn on d1 covers e1. Red's king cannot move. -
The advisor on f2 also cannot go back to e1, because the pawn on d1 would
capture it there. - That advisor on f2 also blocks rank 2, so Red's rook cannot
come to i2 and hit my horse straight away. - If Red does not take the pawn, I
play f2→f1 next, leaving the red king no squares. Then d1→d0 check is strong:
if the advisor captures on d0, horse to c1 is checkmate, barring interference
from Red's rook or cannons. **Why not f2→f1:** Red's advisor takes d2. Then the
rook comes down the i-file and attacks either my horse (on i2) or the pawn on
f1 (on i1). That is worse for me. } 42. 仕五进四 马５退６ { [%emt 0:06:22]
